<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Carries an existing reader's data into the sandbox container the first time
  a Mac App Store build launches. Copied into BibleText.app/Contents/Resources
  by scripts/release-mac-store.sh; macOS reads it ONLY on the launch that
  creates the container.

  WHY THIS FILE DECIDES WHETHER READERS LOSE THEIR NOTES

  A Store build is a different app from the direct download: its own bundle
  path, its own container at
  ~/Library/Containers/uk.co.bibletext.desktop/Data. Under the sandbox
  launchd points HOME at that container, Go's os.UserHomeDir() follows, and
  Fyne builds its preferences path from it — so the app writes a brand-new
  empty preferences file and finds no notes, no reading position, no settings
  and no saved API keys. Nothing is destroyed; the old file simply sits on the
  wrong side of the container boundary.

  The app cannot detect this. readNoteStoreRaw sees an empty store and takes
  the branch its own comment calls "a new reader — genuinely empty, safe to
  write to", and the deliberate-wipe sentinel cannot fire because it lived in
  the file that is now out of reach. So the reader is shown a first-run app
  and their scrapbook — including notes other people shared with them, which
  exist nowhere else — appears to be gone.

  THE PATH IS KEYED "bibletext", NOT THE BUNDLE ID

  app.go calls app.NewWithID("bibletext"), and Fyne's uniqueID takes
  precedence over the FyneApp.toml metadata id when building
  $HOME/Library/Preferences/fyne/<uniqueID>. The directory on disk is
  therefore "fyne/bibletext" while the container is keyed
  "uk.co.bibletext.desktop". A manifest written against the bundle id
  migrates nothing, silently, and there is no second chance.

  COPY, NOT MOVE

  Move would pull the data out from under the direct-download build the
  reader may still be using — two apps can sit in /Applications at once.
  Copy leaves that install working; the two then diverge, which is a product
  decision to communicate rather than a bug (see docs/MAC_APP_STORE.md).

  Caches are deliberately NOT migrated. They are re-downloadable, the app
  already shows progress while refetching, and the embedded Gospels seed
  keeps the reader in scripture meanwhile — so the only cost is bandwidth,
  and copying tens of megabytes into the container to save it is a poor
  trade.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Version</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <key>Migrations</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <!-- Preferences: notes, reading position, text size, red-letter
                 setting, active translation, and any reader-supplied API keys
                 (desktop stores these here, not in the Keychain). -->
            <key>Source</key>
            <string>${HOME}/Library/Preferences/fyne/bibletext</string>
            <key>Destination</key>
            <string>${CONTAINER}/Data/Library/Preferences/fyne/bibletext</string>
            <key>Operation</key>
            <string>Copy</string>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
